rns to the prior -- the T-maze rat starts each run at the centre. Between trials only the Dirichlet counts persist, because those are the model learned structure. The WM has no such boundary: its belief state is carried tick to tick (the R3->R1 recurrence D5e drew), nothing re-initialises from a prior, and no episode ends. So the WM is one unending SPM trial, and within a trial SPM carries beta across time steps exactly as J4 rules. The trial reset is a modelling choice for episodic tasks, not a statement about beta nature, and the WM is not episodic. WHICH GROUND SURVIVES, stated plainly because it is the honest form: the ruling survives on the TICK-IS-A-TIME-STEP ground, and the PARAMETER-TUPLE ground is CONTRADICTED by the reference code. friston2017 puts beta in eta with the parameters that accumulate, but the authors own implementation accumulates every other member of eta across trials and not beta. So beta is a parameter, parameters accumulate is not how the reference treats it. Joe reached the right answer; one of the two arguments for it does not hold. ONE THING THAT BEARS ON IT AND IS NOT IN EITHER SOURCE: the WM has no trial structure at all. It runs a continuous loop of ticks with no boundary at which anything is reset, so there is no natural place to put a reset even if a tick were judged trial-like. That is an argument for carrying that neither the paper nor SPM can supply, and it does not depend on the eta grouping. ", :tags [{:basis {:as-of "2026-09-01", :authority-stale? false, :emitted-at nil, :path "futon2/holes/labs/wm-contract/worklist.edn", :pointer "futon2/holes/labs/wm-contract/worklist.edn:1", :selector [:items :J5 :class], :sha256 "f0f6aedbde868fe2edaafd6a26441b0c86452f05303bbd3be0f169c05e71386d"}, :relation :work-class, :value "J"} {:relation :unmapped, :value "R-node-unmapped"}], :freshness {:as-of "2026-09-08", :basis-count 2, :oldest-basis-as-of "2026-09-01", :state :dated-not-revalidated, :unknown-source-dates? false}, :source-status :